 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If the mesh is planar or a line, the covariance matrix will be singular. The third eigenvalue will be zero (or near-zero). The cross product `first x second` may be unstable if `first` and `second` are nearly parallel. [G] In practice, check for near-zero eigenvalues and handle degenerate cases by defaulting axes to standard basis vectors.
-*   **Precision:** Floating-point errors in covariance calculation can lead to non-orthogonal eigenvectors. Re-orthogonalization (Gram-Schmidt) or using the cross product for the third axis (as done in step 9) mitigates this.
-*   **Sign Ambiguity:** Eigenvectors are defined up to a sign. The flipping steps (6 and 8) resolve this by ensuring the majority of the mesh lies in the positive direction of the principal axes.
